1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,630
Part two: taking a turn. On your turn, choose one of your face-up action cards, do what it says, and then turn that card face down.

2
00:00:08,855 --> 00:00:19,007
Face-down cards can't be used again until you recover them with your Spirit Power card. And if a card tells you to take stones, those stones must actually be available in the bowl.

3
00:00:19,232 --> 00:00:27,061
Take One Stone. Take one stone from the bowl and place it on your board. You have two of these cards. If the bowl is empty, you can't use it.

4
00:00:27,286 --> 00:00:32,875
For example, Ana takes the red stone and gives it to her Rabbit. Then the card is turned face down.

5
00:00:33,100 --> 00:00:40,674
Take Two Stones. Take two stones from the bowl and place them on your board. You can only use it if there are at least two stones in the bowl.

6
00:00:40,899 --> 00:00:49,855
Add Two Stones and Take One. First draw two stones from the bag and add them to the bowl. Then take any one stone from the bowl. More choice, but only one stone.

7
00:00:50,080 --> 00:00:57,259
Add Three Stones and Move Coyote. Draw three stones from the bag into the bowl. You don't take any stones with this card.

8
00:00:57,484 --> 00:01:07,404
Then you may put the Coyote on one animal on your opponent's board. While the Coyote sits there, your opponent can't add any stones to that animal. Moving the Coyote is optional.

9
00:01:07,629 --> 00:01:15,098
The last card is Use Spirit Power and Recover Cards. You may only play it when at least three of your other action cards are face down.

10
00:01:15,323 --> 00:01:24,047
When you play it, choose one of the two face-up Spirit Power cards, do its action in the order written, and slide that card under the bottom of its pile.

11
00:01:24,272 --> 00:01:33,332
Then turn all six of your action cards face up again. Timing this well is the heart of the game: you can't recover too early, but waiting too long wastes turns.

12
00:01:33,557 --> 00:01:42,048
One more rule. Every stone must follow the requirement of the animal you give it to. If you take a stone that you can't place anywhere, it goes back into the bowl.
